  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nFrom Tuesday night through the second half of this week, the \nupper level pattern will undergo a shift. The upper level ridge \nthat is currently centered of Texas and Oklahoma, but sprawls into\nthe southeastern U. S., will gradually shift westward into the \nsouthwestern CONUS, with heights gradually falling over the \nsoutheastern states. So, while the center of the ridge will move \nout of our area, we will still remain generally under the \ninfluence of this upper level high pressure, with temperatures \nremaining well above normal, and even warming a few degrees \ngenerally. With temperatures actually warming slightly between now\nand next weekend, we could make a run at record high temperatures\nFriday and Saturday. \n\nAs has been mentioned in previous discussions, a very weak cold \nfront may approach the area from the north later this week. In \naddition, we will be located within a general weakness compared to\nstronger ridging to our west. With this in mind, have kept some \nlow end (~20%) slight chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nmainly during the afternoon and evening, but confidence remains \nlow in any significant coverage of thunderstorms through the next \nseven days.",
